ee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Conservation Advisory Group","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Licensing Sub-Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Licensing Sub-Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Planning Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Eastbourne Borough Council Cabinet","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Interest rate cuts are deferred; Bank Rate held at 3.75% with next reduction not expected until September 2027, while inflation risks are rising","lead":"The council faces an extended period of elevated borrowing costs. Refinancing of the council's \u00a383.7m external debt and future capital borrowing will occur at rates unlikely to fall in the near term, with downside risk of rates rising instead.","iso":"2026-07-20","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Appendix","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s40562/40562.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"External debt increased by \u00a327 million to \u00a383.7 million; \u00a327 million borrowed in March 2026 for capital programme and land purchase","lead":"The council is significantly expanding its debt position while interest rates are held and unlikely to fall, directly contributing to rising financing costs and increasing the council's interest rate and refinancing risk.","iso":"2026-07-20","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Appendix","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s40562/40562.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"General Fund financing costs increased from 12.6% to 14.7% of net revenue in a single year, a 2.1 percentage point jump","lead":"Rising financing costs reduce the proportion of revenue available for service delivery and signal that the council's debt position is becoming more expensive to maintain relative to its income base.","iso":"2026-07-20","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Appendix","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s40562/40562.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-20","committee":"Lewes District Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Appendix","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s40562/40562.pdf","headline":"Interest rate cuts are deferred; Bank Rate held at 3.75% with next reduction not expected until September 2027, while inflation risks are rising","detail":"The Bank of England paused its easing cycle in December 2025. With rates at 3.75%, no further cuts are forecast until September 2027. Meanwhile, CPI inflation stands at 2.8% and risks rising to 4.5%+ due to Middle East conflict and energy costs, with the possibility that rates may need to rise to control inflation.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The council faces an extended period of elevated borrowing costs. Refinancing of the council's \u00a383.7m external debt and future capital borrowing will occur at rates unlikely to fall in the near term, with downside risk of rates rising instead.","figures":"Bank Rate 3.75%; next cut expected September 2027 to 3.50%; CPI 2.8% April 2026, potential to exceed 4.5%","quote":"A growing risk of inflation is anticipated, meaning interest rates will not be cut for some time, and may increase to counteract inflationary pressures arising from steepening energy costs.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-20","committee":"Lewes District Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Appendix","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s40562/40562.pdf","headline":"External debt increased by \u00a327 million to \u00a383.7 million; \u00a327 million borrowed in March 2026 for capital programme and land purchase","detail":"Total external debt grew from \u00a356.7m to \u00a383.7m. In March 2026, the council borrowed \u00a315m for its 2026/27 capital programme and \u00a312m for a land purchase, accounting for most of the year's debt increase. Long-term PWLB borrowing rose from \u00a341.7m to \u00a353.7m.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The council is significantly expanding its debt position while interest rates are held and unlikely to fall, directly contributing to rising financing costs and increasing the council's interest rate and refinancing risk.","figures":"External debt \u00a356.7m \u2192 \u00a383.7m (47% increase); \u00a315m capital + \u00a312m land purchase in March; PWLB long-term borrowing \u00a341.7m \u2192 \u00a353.7m","quote":"In total, external debt has increased in the year by \u00a327.0m from \u00a356.7m to \u00a383.7m. This includes \u00a315m of debt that was taken out in March 2026 to be used mainly for 2026/27 capital programme and \u00a312m for a land purchase.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-20","committee":"Lewes District Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Treasury Management Appendix","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s40562/40562.pdf","headline":"General Fund financing costs increased from 12.6% to 14.7% of net revenue in a single year, a 2.1 percentage point jump","detail":"The proportion of the council's net revenue consumed by interest and Minimum Revenue Provision (principal repayment) rose significantly. The council budgeted for 12.7% but achieved 14.7%, indicating costs are tracking above forecast and rising faster than revenue growth.","category":"overspend","matters":"Rising financing costs reduce the proportion of revenue available for service delivery and signal that the council's debt position is becoming more expensive to maintain relative to its income base.","figures":"12.6% (2024/25) to 14.7% (2025/26); budgeted 12.7%; Q3 forecast 13.0%","quote":"The proportion of general fund financing costs to net revenue stream has increased by 2.1% from last year to 14.7%.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-20","committee":"Lewes District Council Full Council","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Audit and Governance Committee Annual Report 2025-26 and Appendices","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s40474/40474.pdf","headline":"Local Government Reorganisation for East Sussex creates structural uncertainty for the council, with governance and financial resilience to be monitored through 2026-27.","detail":"The Audit and Governance Committee has identified Local Government Reorganisation (LGR) as a key risk area, should a new unitary authority for East Sussex be progressed. The Committee will seek ongoing assurance over the council's governance arrangements, financial resilience, asset management, internal control and organisational capacity throughout any transition period, including lessons learned from other authorities undergoing LGR.","category":"governance","matters":"Any significant uncertainty over a council's future structure affects its capital planning, operational continuity, and borrowing headroom. A lender needs to understand the timeline, likelihood and implications of potential reorganisation.","figures":"None specified","quote":"Should a new unitary authority for East Sussex be progressed, this will remain a key area of focus during 2026 to 2027 and beyond.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-03-26","committee":"Lewes District Council Full Council","paper_title":"Revised Setting of the 2026-27 Council Tax for Lewes Town Council Area report","paper_url":"https://democracy.lewes-eastbourne.gov.uk/documents/s39121/39121.pdf","headline":"Lewes Town Council precept was calculated incorrectly and required Full Council amendment","detail":"The Lewes Town Council precept contained an error in the Council Tax Report approved on 23 February 2026, requiring this revised Council Tax Resolution to be brought to Full Council for approval. The corrected figures show a Council Tax requirement of \u00a315,531,100 with a basic Band D amount of \u00a3396.48.","category":"governance","matters":"Errors in Council Tax calculations indicate potential weaknesses in the council's financial control processes and accuracy of financial reporting, which is material to a lender's assessment of the council's financial management capability","figures":"Council Tax requirement: \u00a315,531,100; Basic amount (Band D): \u00a3396.48; Special items: \u00a36,792,891","quote":"This paper is being brought to Full Council as the Lewes Town Council Precept was incorrect in the Council's Council Tax Report approved on 23 February 2026.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-20"},"name":"Lewes","findings_total":12,"news_drafts_total":3}}